# Which tests depend on time, for tools/gate.sh and the guard test
# `test_lists::timing_list_matches_the_tests`. One name per line, `module::fn` as
# `cargo test --test all -- --list` prints it. See docs/testing.md.
#
# [timing]  pass or fail can depend on measured CPU or wall time. The gate runs them
#           serially on a quiet machine (step T); every other step skips them.
# [env]     the subset of [timing] that follows REMEDYLUA_TEST_NO_FAST_LOOP for some state
#           it measures (the rest set the loop on each state, or never run the
#           interpreter). Run a second time with the variable set (step Tn).
# [oracle]  the tests only `--features front-oracle` adds or changes (step O).
# [clock-deterministic]  reads a clock (often a fake or counting one) but asserts counts,
#           fuel or results.
# [hang-guard]  fails on time only past a generous 8-60 s bound (a watchdog, the fuzz
#           harness's 10 s C Lua wall and 60 s timeout).
# [time-capped]  time can only end a loop early, under an env cap (REMEDYLUA_SOAK_SECS,
#           REMEDYLUA_S3_SECS, SWEEP*_SECS); without one the loop counts iterations.
# [probe]   ignored exploration helpers that time something and assert nothing on it.
#           These four classes run in the parallel steps.
# [known]   `module::fn FINDING`: a test tied to a finding still open in docs/known-issues.md (gate
#           rule 5). Its step runs it and reports its result, but a failure of it alone
#           does not fail the step. Remove the entry in the commit that closes the
#           finding; the guard fails while it names a finding that is not open.
# [known-windows]  [known], for a gate run on Windows only: the finding shows there and
#           not on the reference system.
#           Any test the guard finds reading a clock must be in one of these sections:
#           a direct `thread_cpu(`, `Instant::now`, `.elapsed(`, `set_time_limit(` or
#           `recv_timeout(`, or a call to a function that does (followed through calls
#           and `use` imports across the test files).

